        "sample": "The escalation of environmental degradation has sparked a debate over who should take charge of mitigating these crises. While some assert that an international organization should bear the sole responsibility rather than individual national governments, I partially disagree with this view. In my opinion, solving environmental problems requires a symbiotic relationship where international bodies provide global frameworks, but national governments actively enforce local measures.\n\nOn the one hand, a centralized international organization is indispensable for tackling transnational environmental threats. Issues such as climate change, ozone layer depletion, and ocean plastic pollution do not respect geographical borders. Therefore, they necessitate coordinated global action. An international entity, like the United Nations, can establish universal environmental standards, set emission reduction targets, and facilitate the transfer of green technologies from developed to developing nations. Without such a governing body, individual countries might prioritize short-term economic gains over long-term ecological sustainability, leading to a fragmented and ineffective response.\n\nOn the other hand, national governments are the crucial agents of execution and enforcement. International organizations lack the direct legislative and executive power to regulate industries or citizens within a sovereign state. Only national governments have the authority to enact strict environmental laws, impose carbon taxes, or subsidize renewable energy projects within their jurisdictions. Furthermore, environmental challenges vary drastically from one region to another; a coastal nation faces different threats compared to a landlocked desert country. Consequently, local governments are best positioned to design and implement tailored strategies that suit their specific geographical, economic, and social contexts.\n\nIn conclusion, while an international organization is essential for orchestrating a unified global strategy and providing support, it cannot replace the executive power of national governments. A sustainable future depends on global cooperation coupled with decisive local action.",
